{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"equivalent-unpacified-loris","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nA convergence test that RE-DERIVES what the send path does — instead of DRIVING the send path\nand capturing what it produced — measures the helper it named, not the convergence it claims.\nIt stays green when the send path drifts, which is the only failure it exists to catch.\n\nThis has now happened **twice in the same module**, and the second time it was caught only\nbecause a code reviewer flagged it as a non-blocking advisory:\n\n1. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` modelled\n   the landed Jira body as `comment_limits.truncate_comment_body(emitted_body)`. When\n   `emersed-specific-mutt` moved the Cloud send path onto `AdfCodec.fit_outbound`, that model\n   went stale and the test kept passing while live convergence was broken — the divergence\n   later filed as `abusive-pebbly-sakimonkey` and paid for in 836 unmarked duplicate comments\n   in live Jira.\n2. The FIRST version of that ticket's own fix carried the same flaw: its oracle computed the\n   send side as `fit_preserving_marker(_decorate_outbound_comment(body), codec.fit_outbound)`\n   — a copy of the composition under test. **It passed Gerrit CI with `Verified +1`.** Seeding\n   `emersed-specific-mutt`'s exact mistake back into `add_comment` left it GREEN.\n\n## Runtime evidence\n\nMutation applied to `acli_cli_ops.add_comment`, reverting it to the drifted form:\n\n```\nbody_arg = json.dumps(codec.to_wire(fit_preserving_marker(body, codec.fit_outbound)))\n       ->  json.dumps(codec.to_wire(codec.fit_outbound(body)))\n\nre-derived oracle : 8 passed     <- blind to the regression it is named for\ncaptured oracle   : 2 failed     <- detects it\n```\n\nThe fix was to capture the `--body` argument from a real `acli_cli_ops.add_comment` call with\nonly `acli_subprocess._run_acli` stubbed, so the send side of the assertion moves with the send\npath rather than with a copy of it.\n\n## Why a ticket and not a comment\n\nNothing in the repo distinguishes the two shapes. Both read as \"the differ agrees with the send\npath\"; only one is true. `make lint`, the complexity ratchet, the mutation gate and the code\nreview all passed the re-derived version, and the review that did notice could only file it as\nadvisory.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide and record the rule for convergence tests in this codebase: the side representing\n      an external effect must be captured from the real call site, not recomputed from the\n      helpers that call site uses.\n- [ ] The rule is discoverable where a test author will hit it (the test-design guidance the\n      debug/implement skills already point at), with the two instances above as worked examples.\n- [ ] Evaluate whether a mechanical check is feasible — e.g. flagging a test that imports the\n      same fit/encode helper the module under test uses on both sides of an equality assertion\n      — and either add it or record why it is not worth the false-positive cost.","id":"4b87-e163-cd9e-43c8","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Convergence tests re-derive the send path instead of driving it"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787312432937795001,"uuid":"877d6bdb-22a2-4031-950c-7ac71f4cbae2"}